text:
When Aluda heard this, he readied the flint for his gun;
He girded on his weapons, he sharpened his sword;
He inspected the hilts, lest the sword's handle should break.

Soon Aluda cast his eye upon the trail of the Kists.
Deftly he leaned over, the rifle let out a sound:
For one thieving Ghilghvelian, an ill moment came;
He falls from his horse, hanging head-downwards;
A bullet wound is on his shoulder, fire catches upon it;
The Ghilghvelian, his comrade slain, pulls back the hammer.
Mutsali’s rifle fired, the cliff’s edge would crumble,
Shreds of lead would shower into Aluda’s lap.

II. The sun has risen in the sky, mists have settled in the ravines;
The giants of the Caucasus lean against the edge of the sky.
Hawks, the slayers of birds, have set out to hunt;
Vultures follow the eagles, attendants seeking a free meal.

The Khevsur young and old turned bitter as wild plums,
They looked upon Aluda with malice, rebuking him harshly:
"Die, death is better for you, why are you a teller of lies;
Unbuckle your snake-edged sword, throw it to the women as a poker;
The shield – for beating the loom, the Khyrim rifle – let them use as a rod;
The pistol will serve them for turning, as a spindle!
You fled from the Kist youth, you have turned into a woman.
You killed him, yet did not cut his right hand, why then did you pursue him?!"

– O men, I desire for you the favor of the lord,
Grant me my fief, and I shall bring you the hand of the enemy.
A raven was carrying prey in its talons, heading for a nook in the cliff.
At that moment I loosed an arrow at it, soaring proudly through the sky;
The shot proved true, it pierced the raven’s wing;
It released the prey from its claws, and it came tumbling down to the earth.

– It is Mutsal's right hand, the cause of the Khevsurs' council,
– Mindia would say in anger, – the cause of their cursing;
I brought it to Aluda, I gave it the color of offense;
When Aluda did not accept it, I showed it the depth of the gorge.
